      "writtenOn": "2022-09-08T17:47:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If link up failed, shouldn\u0027t this be sending BT_ACL_STATE_DISCONNECTED?",
      "revId": "e80437c22492370401dff1a90d46aa3fde035e3d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bf69edba_23f71bb5",
        "filename": "system/btif/src/btif_dm.cc",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 1828,
      "author": {
        "id": 1960535
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-09-12T11:58:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I want to indicate connection failure, therefore sending BT_STATUS_FAIL and BT_ACL_STATE_CONNECTED.\nWe could also send BT_ACL_STATE_DISCONNECTED, but that risks confusing this situation with a disconnection failure. \n\nHowever, currently it is impossible to send BT_STATUS_FAIL and BT_ACL_STATE_DISCONNECTED, because apparently libbluetooth doesn\u0027t check the status on disconnection - it just clean up the connection. I think that is incorrect though, but perhaps is not trivial to fix.\n\nWDYT?",
      "parentUuid": "d1b8cec9_1ad31574",
      "revId": "e80437c22492370401dff1a90d46aa3fde035e3d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a738c120_0d766f37",
        "filename": "system/btif/src/btif_dm.cc",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 1828,
      "author": {
        "id": 1132614
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-09-12T15:49:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think passing the current state is the proper thing to do.  Is there a control block in this file that maintains the current state?  We do have a lot of state machines.  this function could be called in either scenario, so how are you certain it is connected and failed vs disconnected and attempting to connect but failed?\n\nAs for cleaning up disconnect failures...  We clean up the connections and we will invoke a state change callback which will handle telling the application it disconnected.  Doesn\u0027t matter if it was successful or not really.  Because its a disconnection.  Just tell the app \"its done\" and tear down the connection regardless.  Because what can you really do in the event of a disconnection failure?  disconnect harder? exponential backoff disconnect? :-D",
      "parentUuid": "bf69edba_23f71bb5",
      "revId": "e80437c22492370401dff1a90d46aa3fde035e3d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1a484f00_6b700e1b",
        "filename": "system/btif/src/btif_dm.cc",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 1828,
      "author": {
        "id": 1960535
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-09-14T05:00:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Sure, if that\u0027s proper then I\u0027ll change to BT_ACL_STATE_DISCONNECTED.\n\nThis is supposed to be called on connection failure. If something happens when we are already connected, theoretically it shouldn\u0027t call this. Right now, it will call LINK_DOWN_EVT instead, regardless of whether it is caused by failures or otherwise.\n\nI agree that it\u0027s not clear of what to do when disconnection fails. The spec underlines the possibility of disconnection failure though.\n```\nThe HCI_Disconnection_Complete event occurs when a connection is\nterminated. The status parameter indicates if the disconnection was successful\nor not. The reason parameter indicates the reason for the disconnection if the\ndisconnection was successful. If the disconnection was not successful, the\nvalue of the reason parameter shall be ignored by the Host. For example, this\ncan be the case if the Host has issued the HCI_Disconnect command and\nthere was a parameter error, or the command was not presently allowed, or a\nConnection_Handle that didn’t correspond to a connection was given.\n```\nI\u0027m not sure whether this will cause a desync between the host stack and the controller.",
      "parentUuid": "a738c120_0d766f37",
      "revId": "e80437c22492370401dff1a90d46aa3fde035e3d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
